    "body": "A covered conveyor at Mongolia’s Oyu Tolgoi copper-gold mine. Credit: entree Resources\nPosted By: Henry Lazenby November 27, 2026\nEntrée Resources (TSX: ETG; US-OTC: ERLFF) and the Mongolian government have agreed to work towards a deal by the end of March 2027 on two mining licences that have held up underground development at Oyu Tolgoi’s Hugo North Extension copper-gold deposit, the company said Friday.\nAt issue is whether the state takes a stake of up to 34% in Entrée LLC, the subsidiary that holds the Shivee Tolgoi and Javkhlant mining licences, or accepts a royalty instead. Mongolia’s Parliament has the final say. The joint venture property lies about 570 km south of Ulaanbaatar.\nCrews had completed about 230 equivalent metres of lateral development on Shivee Tolgoi by May 30, 2025, a week before Entrée announced the pause. Resolving the dispute so work can resume there would mark “a pivotal moment in Entrée’s 25-year history,” CEO Chris Adams wrote in a Sept. 28 shareholder letter.\nEntrée holds a 20% carried interest in Hugo North Extension. Its partner, mine operator Oyu Tolgoi LLC, needs the licences in its name before crews can resume development there. Work has continued elsewhere at the mine. The operator expects Panel 1, which extends onto Shivee Tolgoi, to be the last section of the first underground level to enter production.\nEntrée shares gained 7¢, or 3.3%, to $2.19 in Toronto at 12:53 p.m. Eastern time Friday, valuing the Vancouver-based company at about $486 million (US$341 million).\nRoyalty proposal\nMongolia already owns 34% of Oyu Tolgoi LLC. Rio Tinto (LSE, ASX, NYSE: RIO) owns the other 66% and expects the open pit and underground mine together to produce an average of 500,000 tonnes of copper a year from 2028 through 2036.\nIn March, Entrée offered to pay the state a royalty on the gross sales value of its share of concentrate. The company said it received no formal response and will prepare a revised offer.\nUnder the terms the two sides agreed to on Nov. 9 and 10, they can extend talks beyond March by mutual consent. Mongolia’s state news agency Montsame reported Thursday that the next round of talks is scheduled for January.\nThe licence transfer also requires tax payment certificates. Mongolia’s tax authority told Entrée LLC it couldn’t issue them because the documents supporting the licence valuations were incomplete, according to Entrée’s third-quarter report.\nUnderground drilling\nEntrée also released assays Friday from seven underground holes its partner drilled at Hugo North Extension between 2023 and 2026. Hole UGD1025A cut 125.3 metres grading 1.28% copper, 0.27 gram gold and 3.18 grams silver per tonne from 362.7 metres downhole. Hole UGD918A, drilled along the mineralized trend, returned 67.6 metres grading 0.95% copper, 0.203 gram gold and 2.19 grams silver per tonne from 438.6 metres.\nCrews drilled from existing workings on the operator’s own licence into the proposed second underground level, known as Lift 2, or the surrounding rock. The intervals are downhole lengths. For holes drilled across the mineralized trend, Entrée estimates true widths at 20% to 70% of the reported lengths.\nEntrée aims to complete an updated technical report on its joint venture interest in the first quarter of 2027. It will incorporate a new resource model for Hugo North, including the extension, and a revised mine plan and production schedule for the first underground level.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Asia\nCOMPANY: Entrée Resources\nRepublish this article",
